From a2e733760218dd5d352c1642ee9da039c8c7277f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 06:42:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Core: owner refusal tested unprivileged; kills leave
 nothing torn or orphaned

The owner checks in dirfd and the store were tested only as root, and CI
runs unprivileged, so the tests returned early and passed. A test can now
give one inode away (`dirfd::theirs::disown`, cfg(test) only): the check
itself runs as in the app, in every `cargo test`, for the store's files,
its directory, and the inbox's target and staging directories.

A write killed before its rename left its `.name.hex.tmp` behind forever;
`Store::open` now removes those, and nothing else. The inbox's copy
fallback created the file under its real name and copied into it, so a
kill mid-copy left a truncated file that looked complete. It now copies
into a staging file in the target's own `.partial` and links it, or
renames it with RENAME_NOREPLACE, into place only once it is whole; the
next open sweeps what a kill left there.

tests/chaos.rs, after clove's ci/chaos.sh, re-runs the test binary as a
writer and SIGKILLs it mid-work, 30 times per part: store writes,
receives placed by link, and receives placed by the copy fallback across
mounts. After every kill the next open must leave every named file whole
and no temporary, staging or copy file; every kill is checked to be what
ended the child, and the kills that landed mid-work are counted and
required. Against the old copy fallback it fails ("f001.bin is torn").

